I think it would be nifty if different shopkeepers had
different abilities and means of preventing theft.

Perhaps one guy will summon a herd of Kops if you steal
anything; perhaps another guy will simply say "Sorry, no
pets allowed" and zap your doggie with a teleportation
wand the instant it appears in the shop.  A third might
nab your backpack if you jump down a hole (as all
shopkeepers currently do); a fourth might have walls
that heal holes (as all shop walls currently do) and curse
you for stealing (and I mean a really good curse, a
professional job). A fifth would have undiggable walls
and a no-teleport zone covering his shop.

Maybe a wizard shopkeeper would have an enchantment working
that caused unpaid items to teleport back to their proper
places in the store after a thousand turns or so -- Next
time you see him he says, "So, if you liked the trial period,
why not consider buying the item?" or "During your trial
period, you used up three charges on this wand that you
still owe me money for..." or even "Now, about your rental
charges..."

An assassin shopkeeper, of course, would send a hired killer
after you if you stole anything. Variations on the theme
include a necromancer shopkeeper sending some kind of undead
after you.

Another shopkeeper is situated so that a very long straight
corridor is the only route away from his shop, and he's got
a nice bow he's an excellent shot with and some nasty arrows.

Yet another has an entryway with a couple of portcullises;
They're mounted so that only one door can be open at a time,
and he never opens the outer one if there's an unpaid item
in the entryway.  Plus, if you kill him, there's nobody to
operate the portcullis controls behind the counter, while
*you* are in the entryway.

You could have a suspicious guy who looks at things this
way; if you're the only person he's seen in the last hundred
turns, and he has less money and/or goods than he had a
hundred turns ago, then he believes you are a thief even if
he doesn't know how you did it, and he calls the Kops/sends
a thug/raises an alarm/curses you/whatever on general
principles.

And so on...

Yes, it would be a large coding job.   You'd need to implement
ten or so new kinds of shopkeeper behavior, pull current anti-
theft behavior out into the same kind of structure, and then
have three or more randomly selected ones turned on in
individual shopkeepers.  The deeper in the dungeon you go, or
the more valuable the goods, the more of the antitheft behaviors
would be turned on.

So stealing from shops would become a bunch of different
puzzles of escalating difficulty (some probably truly without
solution), rather than being *one* puzzle, with a couple of
well-known solutions.

